This paper introduces Iterative Graph Normalization (IGN), a straightforward yet effective algorithm designed to identify the Maximum Weight Independent Set (MWIS) in undirected weighted graphs where edge weights are non-negative. IGN operates as an iterative process that repeatedly applies a local normalization step until convergence, at which point the set of retained nodes constitutes the MWIS. We formally establish the correctness of IGN and demonstrate, through examples, that it strictly outperforms a recently proposed algorithm with similar conceptual foundations. The paper concludes with numerical experiments comparing the efficiency of IGN against other state-of-the-art MWIS algorithms. Our results indicate that IGN is highly competitive, and in several instances, it significantly surpasses existing methods. Notably, IGN is uniquely the only algorithm that reliably delivers optimal solutions for all tested graphs within a reasonable timeframe. All supporting code and data are available at https://github.com/andreafiorini10/IGN. A detailed report, including additional data and an extension of the algorithm capable of handling negative edge weights, can be found in the companion paper \cite{fiorini2021normalization}. Furthermore, we are currently developing an open-source Python package to make the IGN algorithm accessible to the community. We welcome any contributions, bug reports, or suggestions for improvement. Please contact the first author at a.fiorini@unibo.it. We appreciate your interest. Good luck! Best wishes, Andrea Fiorini and Stefano Cremonini. \begin{flushleft} \textit{Keywords:} Maximum Weight Independent Set, Iterative Graph Normalization, Iterative Methods, Local Optimization \end{flushleft} \begin{flushright}
